WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.430
Hollywood is constantly filming bizarre bits,
from space battles, to magic spells, to Nic

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:06.630
Cage's acting.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:11.480
"Ahahaha hahaha haaaa..."

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:15.580
But sometimes, the strange stuff you find
in movies are there completely by accident.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:19.599
Here's a look at five of the weirdest things
accidentally filmed in movies.

00:00:19.599 --> 00:00:21.069
The hanging munchkin

00:00:21.069 --> 00:00:25.190
During a scene in The Wizard of Oz, some viewers
noticed something upsetting going on in the

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:29.859
background: a dark human-shaped figure swinging
from a tree as though it had been hanged.

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:34.040
Rumors soon spread that one of the actors
portraying a Munchkin in the movie was depressed

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:39.340
over his unrequited love for a lady co-star...and
decided to hang himself in the faux woods

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.990
of the Tin Woodsman set.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.490
"Oh, did that hurt?"

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.980
"No, it feels wonderful."

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.970
However, when MGM remastered and re-released
the movie in 1998, instead of the hanging

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.490
figure it appeared as though a large bird
was stretching its wings.

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:58.530
Some conspiracy theorists were convinced there
was a coverup, while others believe a simple

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:02.530
editing error somehow made the bird appear
to look like a human corpse in the original

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:03.530
version.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.680
This is what happens when you try to look
behind the curtain!

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.969
"Pay no attention to that man behind the curtain."

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:11.609
The Candy Man slam

00:01:11.609 --> 00:01:14.430
Who can smack a little girl in the chin and
get away with it?

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.130
The Candy Man can, apparently.

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.569
In the 1971 classic musical adventure Willy
Wonka and the Chocolate Factory, one of the

00:01:20.569 --> 00:01:24.869
extras listening to the candy shop owner's
song got an unexpected surprise when he lifted

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:28.800
the counter-top to let the hungry children
through and accidentally smacked her in the

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.159
chin in the process.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:34.409
Like a pro, the girl didn't react, but man...that
still had to hurt.

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:36.170
The mystery spirit

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:40.159
The Ring is supposed to be scary, but here's
a case of some unintentional terror, as a

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:44.039
shadow person's head and shoulders can be
seen slipping in and out of the lighted window

00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:46.850
frame behind the characters in this sequence.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.889
Chances are this was a simple case of a stagehand
being in the wrong place at the wrong time,

00:01:50.889 --> 00:01:53.039
with the mistake not getting caught by the
editors.

00:01:53.039 --> 00:01:58.179
However, some superstitious fans believe it's
an actual spirit accidentally caught on-screen,

00:01:58.179 --> 00:01:59.639
which seems to happen a lot.

00:01:59.639 --> 00:02:00.799
Maybe even in this YouTube video!

00:02:07.209 --> 00:02:09.070
The missing masterpiece

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:13.010
You wouldn't ordinarily expect a family film
like Stuart Little to accidentally solve a

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:17.890
decades old art mystery, but that's exactly
what happened when Hungarian art expert named

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:22.900
Gergely Barki spotted Robert Bereny's long-lost
avant garde painting, "Sleeping Lady with

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:27.430
Black Vase," (incredible name) in the background
of the 1999 kids comedy.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:32.290
Barki immediately recognized it as a masterpiece
that had been missing since the 1920s and

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:36.030
spent the next two years trying to get in
touch with someone from the movie's production

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.310
crew, only to find out they had sold it off
after filming ended.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:44.030
The collector who bought it later returned
it to Budapest — 90 years after it originally

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:48.420
went missing — and sold it at auction in
2014 for 229,500 Euros.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.760
Makes you think, huh?

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:56.080
"If we get out of this, I'm sticking to painting
and dancing."

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.820
The frozen children

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.710
Will Ferrell might've been trying to trade
in his slapstick comedy schtick for drama

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.100
with 2010's Everything Must Go.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.760
But there was a bit of unintentional humor
in the background of one scene when a bunch

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.960
of children suddenly froze in place while
the adults continued moving and talking.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.350
Either the child actors were told to sit still
for the shot and really nailed it, or something

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.700
went very wrong in the special effects process.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:23.200
Or maybe Laura Dern's character is slyly casting
some sort of stun spell on those poor kids

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.820
with her weird hand gestures?

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.220
Creepy man…

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:27.080
"Stupify"

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.220
Thanks for watching!

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.770
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:37.780
And as always leave us a comment to let us
know which movie accident is your favorite…

